## Prerequisites

The following module surfaces must exist before this plan's Steps 1–4 run.
Each prerequisite is named here as a callable contract this plan consumes;
the prerequisite's internal structure is the prerequisite's plan to decide.

- **`src/service/install/provisionBrain` exports a brain-integration-aware,
  `brainPath`-free orchestrator that returns exactly two InitSteps in fixed
  order — the brain.aide-scaffold step first, the MCP-entry-plan step
  second.** The bundled template helper that powers the brain.aide-scaffold
  step takes no `brainPath` parameter at any layer and emits YAML null at
  every unwired slot. This is the contract from
  `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` — the parallel re-plan owns
  the implementation; cli/init only consumes the two-step output and
  applies the first step. cli/init does NOT pass a brain root path, a
  brain-shaped value of any kind, or any per-host datum into provisionBrain
  through any caller chain.

- **`src/service/install/index.ts` (the `init(...)` entry the install
  service exports) accepts an integration-name input that selects which
  bundled template provisionBrain scaffolds, and routes the value through
  to provisionBrain.** This is a coordinated change in the install service
  entry; the integration name's wire shape (a new field on the input
  object, an additional positional, etc.) is the install service's
  decision. The contract this plan depends on: when cli/init invokes the
  install service entry asking it to plan or apply the brain category for
  a given integration name, the service returns provisionBrain's two-step
  output and applies the brain.aide-scaffold step (or, alternatively,
  returns the planned step shape so cli/init can hand it to the shared
  applySteps helper itself — either factoring is acceptable to this plan,
  see the Decisions section). cli/init never reaches around the install
  service entry to import provisionBrain directly.

- **`src/service/install/applySteps` continues to expose the
  `applySteps(steps): Promise<InitStep[]>` contract and continues to write
  `would-create` brain-category file steps (the brain.aide-scaffold step's
  shape) to disk via `mkdir` + `writeFile`.** This contract is unchanged
  by the rework; cli/init relies on it as a downstream applier when this
  plan's Step 1 routes the brain.aide-scaffold step through it.

- **`src/types/index.ts` continues to export `InitStep`, `InstallResult`,
  and the `BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args: (string | null)[]` typing
  rolled in by the parseBrainAide re-plan.** cli/init never introspects
  `args` (storage-agnostic invariant); the type only matters at the
  shared-writer boundary, where this plan deliberately bypasses any
  read of brain.aide bytes.

If any prerequisite is unmet at build time the implementor escalates back to
the orchestrator before continuing — the cli/init module cannot land its
rewrite on top of stale provisionBrain or install-service shapes.

## Plan

### 1. Replace the inline brain.aide scaffold block with a delegated install-service call

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [x] 1a. In `src/cli/init/index.ts`, change the `runInit` signature from
  `runInit(cwd, write?, options: { brainPath?: string } = {})` to
  `runInit(cwd, write?, options: { brain?: string } = {})`. Update the
  multi-line JSDoc above the function to (i) name the new `options.brain`
  parameter (the integration name; default `"obsidian"`); (ii) state
  there is no `brainPath` parameter on `options` under any rename;
  (iii) describe the new flow as "delegate to the install service to
  plan the brain category, apply only the brain.aide-scaffold InitStep,
  discard the MCP-entry-plan InitStep"; (iv) drop every `<BRAIN_PATH>`
  reference; (v) drop the "always-scaffold contract" framing (the
  scaffold is conditional on absence — the install service's apply
  layer enforces presence-only idempotency).

- [x] 1b. Delete the entire inline brain.aide scaffold block. Concretely
  remove: the `const brainAidePath = join(cwd, ...)` line; the
  `let brainAideExists` flag; the `try { await access(...); } catch {}`
  block; the `brainAideResult: InstallResult = brainAideExists ? ... : await (async () => { ... })()`
  IIFE that reads bytes from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(options.brainPath)`,
  calls `mkdir(...)` + `writeFile(...)`, and emits a `[created]` result.
  Every line that handled brain.aide directly inside `runInit` is gone.

- [x] 1c. Insert a new delegated brain-scaffold pass at the top of
  `runInit`'s body, BEFORE the MCP entry write. Pipeline shape: resolve
  the integration name from `options.brain ?? "obsidian"`; invoke the
  install service entry asking it to plan the brain category for that
  integration (Prerequisite 2); from the returned step list, locate the
  one InitStep whose category is `"brain"` and whose `name` equals the
  brain config name provisionBrain emits (per Prerequisite 1); apply
  ONLY that step via `applySteps`; discard the MCP-entry-plan InitStep
  and any other steps the install service may return alongside it. The
  applied step's status (`would-create` flipped to `created`, or
  pre-existing `exists`) becomes a single `InstallResult` element to
  prepend to the per-file log.
    - The applied-step-to-`InstallResult` mapping: `would-create` →
      `{ status: "created", displayPath: ".aide/config/brain.aide",
      message: "bundled brain template (--brain <integration> default;
      args[<unwired-index>] is YAML null until /aide:brain config fills
      it)" }`. `exists` → `{ status: "exists", displayPath:
      ".aide/config/brain.aide", message: "already present" }`. The
      per-file log line wording is load-bearing for tests in Step 6
      and Step 7; keep it byte-stable across the implementation.
      Specifically, the substring `"already present"` is the existing
      code's wording for `[exists]` lines and must be preserved on the
      brain.aide entry; the substring `"bundled brain template"` is
      the new wording for `[created]` brain.aide lines and is what the
      tests assert against. cli/init does NOT introspect or render the
      args slot index in the message — the literal substring
      `"args[<unwired-index>]"` is template wording, never a computed
      slot index, and the implementor MUST hardcode the spec's
      `args[3]` value for the bundled obsidian default in the
      `[created]` message. cli/init's hardcoding of `3` is acceptable
      ONLY in this user-facing message string (not in any code path
      that reads or writes `mcpServerConfig.args`); the spec's
      no-args-introspection invariant covers code, not log prose.
      Capture this carve-out in the Decisions section (already
      documented below).
    - The cli/init->install-service call MUST forward the integration
      name (`options.brain`) AND nothing else of brain-shape. No
      `brainPath`, no path, no token, no URL, no host-specific datum
      crosses that boundary in either direction. cli/init's only
      output to the install service is the integration name and the
      project root.

- [x] 1d. Update the `results: InstallResult[]` initialization. The
  previous shape was `[brainAideResult, mcpResult]`; the new shape is
  `[brainScaffoldResult, mcpResult]` — the same ordering invariant
  (brain.aide first, MCP entry second), now sourced from the
  delegated apply path. The applied-step-to-result mapping in 1c is
  the only computation; nothing reads bytes off disk after the
  applySteps call returns.

- [x] 1e. Replace the call site `const mcpRaw = await
  writeMcpEntry(cwd, options.brainPath)` with `const mcpRaw = await
  writeMcpEntry(cwd)` (single positional argument). Step 2 covers the
  helper itself.

- [x] 1f. Remove now-unused imports from `src/cli/init/index.ts`. The
  `import obsidianBrainAideTemplate ...` line goes. From
  `node:fs/promises`: `writeFile`, `access`, and `mkdir` were only used
  by the deleted scaffold block — drop them. From `node:path`: keep
  `join` (still used downstream), drop `dirname` (only used by the
  scaffold block). Add the new imports needed for the delegated path:
  the install service entry from `@/service/install/index.js` and
  `applySteps` from `@/service/install/applySteps/index.js`. The
  precise import names follow whatever Prerequisite 2 settles; do not
  invent symbols — match the install service's actual export shape.

### 2. Strip brain logic out of `writeMcpEntry`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [x] 2a. In `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts`, change the function
  signature from `writeMcpEntry(projectRoot: string, brainPath?: string)`
  to `writeMcpEntry(projectRoot: string): Promise<WriteMcpEntryResult>`.
  The second positional parameter is gone; no replacement parameter
  takes its place under any rename.

- [x] 2b. Delete the entire brain-derivation section from the function
  body: the `brainAidePath = join(...)` line; the
  `try { brainAideContent = await readFile(...); } catch (err) { ... }`
  block (and its `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(brainPath)` ENOENT
  fallback); the `parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideContent)` call; the
  throw on a non-`ok` parse result; the construction of `brainEntry`
  via `interpolateArgs(...)`; the assignment `entries.brain =
  brainEntry`; and the assignment `entries.obsidian = "delete"`. After
  these deletions, the `entries` map is exactly one literal:
  `const entries: Record<string, McpServerEntry | "delete"> = { aide: mcpEntry() }`.

- [x] 2c. Drop the now-unused imports from `writeMcpEntry/index.ts`:
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`, `parseBrainAideFromString`, and
  `interpolateArgs`. Drop `readFile` from `node:fs/promises` if and
  only if the preserved-server-count re-read no longer uses it (it
  does — keep `readFile` for that). Drop `join` from `node:path` if
  no surviving line still uses it (the preserved-count re-read
  computes `join(projectRoot, ".mcp.json")` — keep `join`). Keep
  `mcpEntry` from `wireMcp` and `sharedWriteMcpEntry` from
  `@/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry`. Keep `McpServerEntry` from `@/types`
  if any annotation still uses it; drop if not.

- [x] 2d. Update the message-composition logic at the bottom of the
  function. Today it produces strings like
  `"aide and brain MCP server entries; removed legacy obsidian keys"`
  with optional preserved-count suffix. With only the `aide` entry in
  play and no legacy migration:
    - `unchanged` branch → return
      `{ status: "exists", message: "aide MCP server entry already configured" }`.
    - Write branch → `written.length === 1` always (only `"aide"`),
      `deleted.length === 0` always (no `obsidian: "delete"` is sent).
      Compose the singular form `"aide MCP server entry"`. Keep the
      existing preserved-count suffix logic — re-read `.mcp.json`,
      filter the `mcpServers` map by the new `managedKeys` set
      `new Set(["aide", "aidemd-mcp"])` (drop `"brain"` and
      `"obsidian"`), count the rest, and append
      ` (merged with N existing server${N === 1 ? "" : "s"})` when
      `N > 0`. Final message shapes:
      `"aide MCP server entry"` (no preserved servers) or
      `"aide MCP server entry (merged with 2 existing servers)"`
      (two preserved). NO `(merged with 0 existing servers)` ever
      lands — the suffix is conditional on `N > 0`.

- [x] 2e. Rewrite the JSDoc on the default export. The new JSDoc must
  describe:
    - The helper writes ONLY the `aide` key into `.mcp.json` via the
      shared additive-merge writer.
    - It NEVER reads `.aide/config/brain.aide`, NEVER derives a brain
      entry, NEVER writes a `brain` key, NEVER writes or deletes a
      legacy `obsidian` key. cli/init does not migrate legacy keys;
      sync is the only mutation surface for `mcpServers.brain`, and
      pre-rework hosts carry a legacy `obsidian` key as user data
      forever, untouched by cli/init.
    - It throws on malformed `.mcp.json` (the only abort trigger for
      cli/init).
    - It is idempotent: re-running on a project where the `aide`
      entry is already present returns
      `{ status: "exists", ... }` with no write.
    - The `WriteMcpEntryResult` shape stays exported as today.

### 3. Rewrite `parseBrain`, `deferredCategories`, and the IIFE flag-parsing surface

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/foundations/anti-patterns`

- [x] 3a. Delete the existing `parseBrainPath(argv)` function from
  `src/cli/init/index.ts` entirely. The `--brain-path` family of flags
  is forbidden by the new spec; no successor parser reads
  backend-specific values from argv.

- [x] 3b. Add a new `parseBrain(argv: readonly string[]): string` helper
  to `src/cli/init/index.ts`. Contract:
    - Returns the integration name selected by `--brain <name>` /
      `--brain=<name>`, defaulting to `"obsidian"` when the flag is
      absent.
    - Empty value (e.g. `--brain ""` or `--brain` followed by no
      token) → throw a clear `Error` whose message names the missing
      value and lists the registered integrations.
    - Unknown value (e.g. `--brain mem0` while only `obsidian` is
      registered) → throw a clear `Error` whose message names the
      offending value AND lists the registered integrations the user
      can choose from.
    - The validated set of integration names lives in a single
      module-local constant — the `BRAIN_INTEGRATIONS` registry — see
      Decisions section for where the registry lives. Today the
      registry has exactly one entry: `"obsidian"`.
    - The helper does NOT read any backend-specific value off argv.
      It accepts only the integration name token. Anything else
      passes through to the forbidden-flag check (Step 3c) or is
      ignored.

- [x] 3c. Add a new `assertNoForbiddenFlags(argv: readonly string[]):
  void` helper to `src/cli/init/index.ts`. Contract:
    - Scans argv for any token in the forbidden-flag set:
      `--brain-path`, `--brain-path=<value>`, `--vault-path`,
      `--vault-path=<value>`, `--brain-root`, `--brain-root=<value>`,
      `--brain-token`, `--brain-token=<value>`, `--brain-url`,
      `--brain-url=<value>`, `--brain-name`, `--brain-name=<value>`.
      Match both the bare-flag form and the `=<value>` form for each.
    - On detection, throw a clear `Error` whose message names the
      offending flag verbatim AND routes the user to
      `/aide:brain config` for backend-value-collecting concerns.
      Suggested wording (test-asserted): `"Backend-specific flag
      <flag> is not accepted at install time. Run /aide:brain config
      inside Claude Code on the first /aide run to wire the brain."`
    - The set of forbidden flag names is itself a module-local
      constant (`FORBIDDEN_BRAIN_FLAGS`) so future additions are a
      single-place change.
    - The helper does NOT consume `--brain` (without a hyphen suffix)
      — that is `parseBrain`'s flag.
    - The helper does NOT swallow errors silently; failure is the
      contract.

- [x] 3d. Rewrite `deferredCategories(...)` in `src/cli/init/index.ts`
  to take no parameters. The new body returns a fixed two-item array
  in this order:
    1. `"Brain wiring — open Claude Code and run /aide; on the first
       run, /aide:brain config will fill the unwired slot in
       .aide/config/brain.aide, derive the brain MCP entry through
       cli/sync, and seed the four entry-point artifacts into your
       brain."`
    2. `"IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>"`

    The brain entry's exact wording is load-bearing for the
    regression guards in Step 6 and the integration-test stdout
    assertions in Step 7. The IDE entry's exact wording is preserved
    byte-for-byte from the current implementation (the `--ide`
    flag-parsing gap is an existing todo.aide carry-forward and is
    NOT closed by this plan; see Decisions).

- [x] 3e. Update the JSDoc above `deferredCategories` to describe the
  new contract:
    - Brain wiring is permanently routed to `/aide:brain config` on
      the first `/aide` run regardless of CLI flags or disk state —
      cli/init does no brain wiring beyond scaffolding the bundled
      template.
    - The brain entry's prose names `/aide` as the literal command
      the user types in Claude Code, then `/aide:brain config` as
      the surface that does the wiring. Naming `/aide` itself as the
      *wiring* surface (the orchestrator does not own brain-wiring
      inline recovery) is the regression class the entry guards
      against. The shipped string MUST name `/aide:brain config` as
      the wiring surface — that is the regression guard the spec's
      `outcomes.undesired` "directs the user to a removed slash
      command" entry forbids violating.
    - Cite the spec's `outcomes.undesired` "directs the user to a
      removed slash command" entry as the rule guarded against.

- [x] 3f. Update the `renderWarning(...)` call site near the bottom of
  `runInit` so the `deferredCategories` field receives
  `deferredCategories()` (no arguments). Drop any `options.brainPath`
  reference at this call site.

- [x] 3g. Rewrite the IIFE at the bottom of `index.ts`. Sequence:
    1. `--help` early-exit branch (rewritten in Step 4) runs first.
    2. Call `assertNoForbiddenFlags(process.argv.slice(2))`. On throw,
       write the error message to stderr via
       `process.stderr.write(...)` and `process.exit(1)`. No
       partial-state files are written when this fires.
    3. Call `parseBrain(process.argv.slice(2))` to resolve the
       integration name. On throw, same stderr-and-exit-1 path.
    4. `await runInit(process.cwd(), undefined, { brain })`.
    5. Existing `try ... catch` block survives — malformed
       `.mcp.json` still maps to a stderr line and `process.exit(1)`.
  The `--ide <choice>` flag continues to flow through argv unparsed
  (the existing todo.aide gap; this plan does not touch IDE wiring).

