    - The per-file log starts with the brain.aide result, then the
      MCP entry result, then every methodology planner's results.
    - `deferredCategories()` is parameter-less and returns the
      two-item array.
    - `parseBrain` and `assertNoForbiddenFlags` exist and are
      invoked from the IIFE in the order Step 3g specifies.
    - The `--help` text lists no `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`,
      etc. and routes brain wiring to `/aide:brain config` on first
      `/aide` run.
    - The IIFE catches throws and exits non-zero, with no behavior
      change to the malformed-JSON path.

- [x] 10e. Run `rtk vitest run src/cli/init/index.integration.test.ts`
  in isolation. Failures here typically mean the install service's
  brain category did not land the brain.aide file at the expected
  path or `runInit`'s orchestration order broke (the brain-scaffold
  result must precede the MCP result in the per-file log). Trace
  the failure to the specific orchestration site, not to a
  brain-related cause inside cli/init.

## Decisions

- **The BRAIN_INTEGRATIONS registry is module-local to `cli/init/index.ts`,
  not extracted into the install service or provisionBrain.** cli/init's
  job is argument parsing — it needs to know the registered integration
  set to validate `--brain <name>`. provisionBrain's job is to scaffold
  whichever bundled template the install service hands it; provisionBrain
  doesn't validate names. The install service's job is to route the
  integration name into provisionBrain; it doesn't validate names either
  (validation happens at the cli/init argument boundary, before the
  install service is called). Putting the registry in cli/init keeps the
  validation logic at the boundary where the user error originates and
  avoids requiring the install service to teach cli/init what its
  registered set is. The registry is a single-line constant
  (`const BRAIN_INTEGRATIONS = ["obsidian"] as const`) with one ergonomic
  consequence: future integrations land their template helper in
  provisionBrain (or wherever the install service's brain category
  sources its bundled bytes), then add their name to this constant.
  Two-place change, both small, both adjacent to the surface they
  affect.

- **cli/init delegates to the install service for the brain.aide
  scaffold, NOT directly to provisionBrain.** Two reasons. First, the
  install service is the consumer-agnostic core (the parent spec is
  explicit: "the same service is invoked by the `aide_init` MCP tool
  and by the npx CLI"); cli/init reaching past the install service to
  invoke provisionBrain directly would re-couple cli/init to
  provisionBrain's internal contract and break the
  one-shared-service-two-thin-consumers shape. Second, the install
  service already owns the apply layer — when cli/init asks for
  `category: "brain"`, the install service plans provisionBrain's two
  steps and applies them through the standard apply pipeline. cli/init
  needs to filter out the MCP-entry-plan step before it gets applied;
  that filtering is the only cli/init-specific brain logic. The exact
  shape of the install-service-side filter (a new `category: "brain.aide"`
  sub-category, an explicit "apply only step 1" knob on the input, or a
  return shape that lets cli/init pull the planned step and hand it to
  applySteps directly) is the install service's plan to settle. This
  plan documents the contract cli/init needs (brain.aide-scaffold step
  applies; MCP-entry-plan step does NOT apply) and leaves the
  install-service factoring to its own re-plan.

- **The `args[3]` substring in the brain.aide `[created]` log message
  is hardcoded user-facing prose, NOT an introspection of
  `mcpServerConfig.args`.** The spec's no-args-introspection invariant
  forbids cli/init from reading `args[N]` for any N to extract
  semantics. The log message
  `"bundled brain template (--brain obsidian default; args[3] is YAML null until /aide:brain config fills it)"`
  contains the literal substring `args[3]`, but cli/init never reads
  the actual args[3] value from the scaffolded brain.aide — the
  substring is a fixed user-facing pointer telling the user where the
  unwired slot lives in the bundled obsidian default. The slot index
  is documented in the integration's own `config` body section (per
  the architectural-intent #25 invariant); cli/init's log message
  mirrors that documentation for the bundled obsidian default's
  benefit. Future integrations whose path slot lives at a different
  index would hand the log message a different fixed substring (or
  drop it entirely if they have no path-shaped slot). The
  implementor's check: cli/init's source must contain ZERO code paths
  that read `mcpServerConfig.args`, even via parser results from the
  install service; the literal `3` appears only in a user-facing
  string constant.

- **Drop legacy `obsidian`-key migration from cli/init entirely.** The
  current `writeMcpEntry` wraps `entries.obsidian = "delete"` to
  remove a legacy `obsidian` MCP entry from pre-rework installs. After
  this plan, cli/init does not touch the `obsidian` key under any
  circumstance — it survives in the user's `.mcp.json` as user data.
  Rationale: the storage-agnostic invariant says cli/init never
  branches on a backend's name, and adding `obsidian` to the deletion
  set is a name-aware code path. The handoff (architectural-intent #26)
  is explicit: legacy `mcpServers.obsidian` migration is dropped
  entirely from this package; pre-rework hosts keep their `obsidian`
  key as user data forever. The migration is removed from cli/init
  AND from cli/sync; no follow-up plan recovers it. This Decision
  supersedes the prior plan's "moves to cli/sync" wording.

- **`cli/init` does NOT close the `--ide` flag-parsing gap or the
  `[exists]`-in-warning gap; both stay as todo.aide carry-forwards.**
  The delegation explicitly scopes this plan to the brain-wiring
  rework. Both gaps are real misalignments against the spec's good
  example, but their fixes are isolated and out of scope:
    - `[exists]`-in-warning: a one-line filter change inside
      `runInit`'s warning-input construction; affects multiple
      existing tests; would re-shape Step 6 in ways the delegation
      did not approve. Preserving it as `todo.aide` item 1 keeps the
      plan's scope tight; the follow-up plan that addresses it is
      small and isolated.
    - `--ide` flag-parsing: a new `parseIde(argv)` function, an
      IDE-application step in `runInit`, and a
      `deferredCategories(ide?)` signature that omits the IDE entry
      when `--ide` was supplied. Implementing this requires a
      design pass on Zed/VS Code/Cursor file targets, the
      IDE-application step's failure mode, and the warning's
      IDE-omission behavior — all of which are out of scope. The
      IDE deferral string in this plan stays exactly as today's
      code emits it.
  Both gaps are tracked under `todo.aide`'s `## Issues` section, and
  Step 9b's retro entry calls them out as still-open.

- **`renderWarning` is unchanged in shape; only its input strings
  change.** The renderer is data-driven by design — input strings
  from `deferredCategories()` flow through `runInit` into
  `renderWarning` verbatim. That separation is what makes this
  rewrite a string-table swap rather than a renderer change. Step 8b
  is documentation-only.

- **Tests stay split between mocked unit (`index.test.ts`) and
  real-FS integration (`index.integration.test.ts`).** Both files
  exist already, the split matches the testing playbook's two-tier
  model, and the contract change is large enough that mechanical
  fixture replacement would leave dead code and stale describe
  blocks. Both files are full rewrites in this plan.

- **The brain-wiring deferred string names `/aide` as the user-typed
  entry point, then `/aide:brain config` as the wiring surface.**
  This is the exact shape the spec's good-example output uses (line
  252). The retired anti-pattern is naming `/aide` as the *wiring*
  surface itself (e.g. "the orchestrator will detect that your brain
  is not wired and walk you through configuration") — that violates
  the "directs the user to a removed slash command" undesired outcome
  because `/aide` does not own brain-wiring inline recovery; it
  forwards to `/aide:brain config`. The new string is "open Claude
  Code and run /aide; on the first run, /aide:brain config will fill
  the unwired slot in .aide/config/brain.aide, derive the brain MCP
  entry through cli/sync, and seed the four entry-point artifacts
  into your brain." Both surfaces ship; the user's fingers type one;
  the agent invokes the other. The regression guard in Step 6d-x
  asserts the new prose is present and the retired prose is not.

- **`writeMcpEntry`'s preserved-count logic stays.** The current
  helper re-reads `.mcp.json` after writing to count non-managed
  servers and includes the count in the result message. This plan
  keeps that logic but updates the `managedKeys` set to drop `brain`
  and `obsidian` — neither key is managed by cli/init under the new
  contract. A pre-existing `obsidian` or `brain` key in the user's
  `.mcp.json` counts as a non-managed pre-existing server in the
  preserved-count message; the user-visible message stays informative
  ("aide MCP server entry (merged with 1 existing server)").

- **No new helpers in this scope.** The plan reuses every existing
  import that survives the brain-removal pruning. No new
  orchestrator-level helper is needed beyond `parseBrain` and
  `assertNoForbiddenFlags`, which replace the deleted `parseBrainPath`
  one-for-one. Adding a helper to "branch the message" or "compose
  the deferred array" would violate the playbook's
  "extract helpers into subfolders only when they have independent
  reasons to exist" rule (`coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`).
  `parseBrain` and `assertNoForbiddenFlags` stay at the top of
  `index.ts` next to the IIFE; both are small, both are
  argv-parsing-specific, and neither is consumed elsewhere in the
  module. If they grow, they can be extracted into their own
  subfolders later.

- **The forbidden-flag check is a hard fail, not a warn-and-continue.**
  The cli/init spec's `outcomes.undesired` is explicit that any
  brain-shaped flag whose value is a backend-specific datum is
  forbidden by the whole shape, not a list of names. A warn-and-continue
  posture would let the user supply `--brain-path /foo`, see a
  warning, and assume the path was somehow honored — a worse
  experience than an immediate failure with a clear pointer to
  `/aide:brain config`. The hard-fail also closes the door on a
  future regression where someone adds back a "soft-warn for
  back-compat" branch and the value silently flows into the install
  service. Hard fail at the argv boundary, before the install service
  is touched, is the only safe shape.
